Here is a Java solution that uses logarithms to solve the problem without loops or recursion.

```java
public class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }
        while (n % 3 == 0) {
            n /= 3;
        }
        return n == 1;
    }
}
```

However, the above solution is not allowed as it uses a while loop. Here is a solution that uses logarithms to solve the problem without loops or recursion.

```java
import java.lang.Math;

public class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }
        return Math.log10(n) / Math.log10(3) % 1 == 0;
    }
}
```

This solution works by calculating the logarithm base 3 of the input number. If the result is an integer, then the number is a power of 3. The modulo operation is used to check if the result is an integer. If the result is not an integer, then the number is not a power of 3. 

This solution has a time complexity of O(1) and a space complexity of O(1), making it efficient for large inputs. 

Note: The above solution assumes that the input is an integer. If the input can be a float or double, you may need to use a different approach. 

Also, the above solution does not handle the case where the input is a very large number that exceeds the maximum value that can be represented by a double in Java. In such cases, you may need to use a library that supports arbitrary-precision arithmetic. 

Here is an example of how you can use the above solution:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Solution solution = new Solution();
        System.out.println(solution.isPowerOfThree(27));  // prints: true
        System.out.println(solution.isPowerOfThree(0));  // prints: false
        System.out.println(solution.isPowerOfThree(-1)); // prints: false
    }
}
```

