    std::ifstream file(filename);
    if (!file.is_open()) {
        std::cerr << "Error: Unable to open file " << filename << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    // Initialize a map to store word counts
    std::map<std::string, int> wordCounts;

    // Read the file line by line
    std::string line;
    while (std::getline(file, line)) {
        // Convert the line to lowercase
        for (char& c : line) {
            c = std::tolower(c);
        }

        // Split the line into words
        std::istringstream iss(line);
        std::string wordInLine;
        while (iss >> wordInLine) {
            // Remove punctuation and special characters
            wordInLine.erase(std::remove_if(wordInLine.begin(), wordInLine.end(), [](char c) { return !std::isalnum(c); }), wordInLine.end());

            // Increment the word count
            if (!wordInLine.empty()) {
                ++wordCounts[wordInLine];
            }
        }
    }

    // Print the word count
    if (wordCounts.find(word) != wordCounts.end()) {
        std::cout << "The word '" << word << "' appears " << wordCounts[word] << " times in the file." << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "The word '" << word << "' does not appear in the file." << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

**Explanation**
-------------

1. The program opens the specified file in read mode and checks if it was successful. If not, it prints an error message and exits.
2. It initializes an empty map `wordCounts` to store the word counts.
3. The program reads the file line by line using `std::getline`.
4. For each line, it converts the text to lowercase using a loop and `std::tolower`.
5. It splits the line into individual words using `std::istringstream` and `>>`.
6. For each word, it removes punctuation and special characters using `std::remove_if` and `std::isalnum`.
7. If the word is not empty, it increments the corresponding count in the `wordCounts` map.
8. After processing the entire file, it checks if the specified word is in the map. If it is, it prints the count. Otherwise, it prints a message indicating that the word was not found.

**Example Use Case**
-------------------

Suppose you have a file `example.txt` containing the following text:
```
This is an example sentence.
This sentence is an example.
The word "example" appears multiple times.
```
If you run the program with `filename = "example.txt"` and `word = "example"`, the output will be:
```
The word 'example' appears 3 times in the file.
```
Note that this program is case-insensitive and ignores punctuation and special characters. If you want to modify the behavior, you can adjust the code accordingly.

